Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hello,

I am working on an age-structured Jolly Seber model in marginalized multistate parameterization (following Saracco & Yackulic 2023) which is a based on the multistate JS described in Kery & Schaub (2011).  My question relates specifically to defining the priors on entry (transition) probability within this age-structured framework.  Here's a generalized description of the problem:

Some critters exist in latent states (not-yet-entered, age-1, age-2, age-3, dead) with four observed states (seen as age-1, seen as age-2, seen as age-3, not observed).  Ages are known for all observed individuals.  All critters start in a dummy occasion in "not-yet-entered" state and then enter the observed states during sampling occasions.  Individuals can enter the population at any age.  To disregard the age-stratification briefly, entry probability at time t could be described in JAGS with a uniform distribution for simplicity as:

for (t in 1:(n.occasions-1)) {
gamma[t] ~ dunif(0, 1)
}

However, this entry probability describes the probability of entering the population inclusive of being in states age-1, age-2, or age-3.  The probability of entering the focal population at one of these given ages must be some component of the overall probability currently described in gamma above.  I have sought to define state-specific entry probabilities through time with an added dimension:

for (t in 1:(n.occasions-1)) {
for (s in 1:n.states) {
gamma[s, t] ~ # ?????
}
}

The distribution used for state-specific priors is especially important for determining the probability that an individual remains in the not-yet-entered (NYE) state at time t in a latent-state transition matrix:

for (t in 1:(n.occasions-1)) {
omega[NYE, NYE, t] <- 1 - sum(gamma[1:3, t])
}

How could an uninformative prior on time-specific entry be spread across all latent states such that the time-specific probability of entry does not exceed 1?  Paper references or other demo sources appreciated.

Cheers,
Joshua

