Following up, as my initial problem has seemingly been solved by an off-list suggestion to use the Dirichlet, but I am still held up in derivation of these state-specific abundances.  I believe my issue is wrapped up in the mechanics of the removal-entry parameter once multiple "alive" states are considered.  A summary of my thoughts and code snippets below, but an example model with these components is attached.

States Description:
There are four observed states (alive @ age classes 1 to 3) and five latent states(not yet entered, observed states (n=3), dead).  Individuals will deterministically progress through age classes (they correspond to actual ages), but they don't line up with observation occasions so an individual can be observed in a single age class multiple times.

Given this, I've set up the following prior on entry-removal, gamma, as individuals may enter the population at any age class, but the sum of entry probabilities must necessarily sum to one.

prior.vec <- rep(1/n.states, n.states) # in jags data

for (t in 1:(n.occ-1)) {
      gamma[1:n.states, t] ~ ddirch(prior.vec[]) # jags prior
}

I begin to feel uncertain now with calculating the negative probability of entry "qgamma" and conditional entry probabilities "c", however, once this additional state-dimension is added.  Here's my rationale with both:

"Qgamma" represents the probability of not entering into a given state at the given timestep, therefore:

for (t in 1:(n.occasions-1)) {
      for (s in 1:n.states) {

            qgamma[s, t] <- 1 - gamma[s, t]
      } #s
} #t

"C" represents the probability that an individual has not previously entered the population in any state.  This then follows:

for (s in 1:n.states) {              # time = 1
      c[s, 1] <- gamma[s, 1]
} #s

for (t in 2:(n.occasions-1) {
      qgamma_prime[t] <- sum(qgamma[1:n.states, t])  # not previously entered in any state at a given time
} #t

for (t in 2:(n.occasions-1)) {

      for (s in 1:n.states) {

            c[s, t] <- gamma[s, t] * prod(qgamma_prime[1:(t=1)])
      } #s
} #t

It then follows that psi, the inclusion probability of augmented and observed individuals in a given state is:

for (s in 1:n.states) {

      psi[s] <- sum(c[s, 1:(n.occasions-1)])
} #s

with unconditional time-specific entry probabilities by state being:

for (t in 1:(n.occasions-1)) {

      b[s, t] <- c[s, t] / psi[s]
      B[s, t] <- c[s, t] * M
} #t

However, this is code as presented above is plagued by runtime errors relating to calculation of b and B for which I have not yet been able to determine the cause.  Previously, I attempted to run without the qgamma_prime consolidation; the model would run without specifying  qgamma to mean "no previous entry in any state, but this resulted in inclusion estimates exceeding 99% even with astronomical data augmentation rates.  An example code outlining the points I've made is attached.

If anyone is aware of examples of published multistate-esque models which may be helpful in diagnosing and correcting my error here, I appreciate your reference to them as well.

